    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_8c0a4e377143b35179d870a94ca57b886b562c31d169f2fae05c156bc0c3f746",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Least-counterexample descent forces P(n)<log n. Deleting the largest prime produces a cofactor certified by minimality after finite exceptions are discharged; if the deleted prime were at least log n, the multiplicative increase 1+1/q would be too small relative to the increase in log log to restore failure.",
